{"assertion":{"kind":"theoretical","text":"Erdős Problem #1003: declared status 'open'. Formalized: yes. Are there infinitely many solutions to $\\phi(n)=\\phi(n+1)$, where $\\phi$ is the Euler totient function? Current best: Erd\\H{o}s \\cite{Er85e} says that, presumably, for every $k\\geq 1$ the equation\\[\\phi(n)=\\phi(n+1)=\\cdots=\\phi(n+k)\\]has infinitely many solutions. Erd\\H{o}s, Pomerance, and S\\'{a}rk\\\"{o}zy \\cite{EPS87} proved that the number of $n\\leq x$ with $\\phi(n)=\\phi(n+1)$ is at most\\[\\frac{x}{\\exp((\\log x)^{1/3})}.\\]See [946] for the analogous question with the divisor function. Prize: no. OEIS: A001274. Tags: number theory."},"claim_id":"vcl_ac4f127d18a869ebf39f9fc9525e069b43d08e83eab2d325ab2d7ab4eb914fc9","conditions":[],"created_at":"2026-06-16T00:00:00Z","evidence":[],"extensions":{"vela.legacy-finding.v1":{"annotations":[],"attachments":[],"confidence":{"basis":"external catalogue record","extraction_confidence":1.0,"kind":"frontier_epistemic","method":"llm_initial","score":0.5},"flags":{"contested":false,"declining":false,"gap":false,"gravity_well":false,"negative_space":false,"retracted":false},"legacy_conditions":{"duration":null,"text":""},"legacy_evidence":{"evidence_spans":[],"method":"erdos_deep","model_system":"n/a","replicated":false,"replication_count":null,"type":"theoretical"},"legacy_links":[],"legacy_provenance":{"authors":[],"doi":null,"extraction":{"extracted_at":"","extractor_version":"","method":"database_import","model":null,"model_version":null},"review":null,"source_type":"database_record","title":"erdos_deep:1003","year":null},"previous_version":null,"source_schema":"https://vela.science/schema/finding-bundle/v0.10.0","source_version":1,"updated":null}},"imported_from":{"era":"finding_v0_10","object_id":"vf_6fea18f0d1fafdce","object_root":"sha256:80a343a049faed421c57cb9d3efa7901ff88bb6193c7cc9507a3147402bff20d","predecessor_commit":"c236b4fdedb25e0d726e24e2780d50962e4844f7"},"provenance":[{"kind":"database